### TITLE ###
Pre-flight lists every prompt and runs nothing
### INPUT ###
print("side effect")
ok = confirm("Deploy?")
notes = input("Notes")
where = pick(["dev", "prod"])
print(ok, notes, where)
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDERR ###
error[RAD20046]: this script needs to ask you something, but there's no terminal

  TestCase prompts in 3 places. rad found no terminal - stdin isn't one and /dev/tty isn't available
  - so it can't ask. Supply the answers up front, keyed by line number.

  TestCase:2  confirm "Deploy?"
  TestCase:3  input   "Notes"
  TestCase:4  pick    options: dev, prod

    rad TestCase --color=never --reply '2:<yes|no>' --reply '3:<value>' --reply '4:<option>'

  Every <...> is a blank - rad writes the shape of the command, never the answers. Fill each one in;
  rad takes the text literally and matches it exactly. For a prompt on a branch you know won't run,
  or a filtered pick you expect to settle itself, use --reply-na instead of choosing for it.
  = info: rad docs RAD20046
### EXIT ###
7

### TITLE ###
Answers supplied for every prompt let the script run
### INPUT ###
ok = confirm("Deploy?")
notes = input("Notes")
where = pick(["dev", "prod"])
print(ok, notes, where)
### ARGS ###
--reply
1:yes
--reply
2:all good
--reply
3:prod
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDOUT ###
true all good prod

### TITLE ###
An answer that is not a known option is rejected up front
### INPUT ###
where = pick(["dev", "prod"])
print(where)
### ARGS ###
--reply
1:staging
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDERR ###
error[RAD20046]: "staging" is not one of the options for the pick at 1. Valid options: dev, prod.
  Answers must match exactly - rad won't guess which one you meant
  = info: rad docs RAD20046
### EXIT ###
7

### TITLE ###
A key pointing at no prompt names the real ones
### INPUT ###
ok = confirm("Deploy?")
print(ok)
### ARGS ###
--reply
9:yes
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDERR ###
error[RAD20046]: no prompt at 9. This script prompts at: 1. If the script changed, re-run it without
  --reply to get the current list
  = info: rad docs RAD20046
### EXIT ###
7

### TITLE ###
reply-na satisfies pre-flight for a branch that is not taken
### INPUT ###
if false:
    ok = confirm("Never asked?")
    print(ok)
print("done")
### ARGS ###
--reply-na
2
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDOUT ###
done

### TITLE ###
Reaching an reply-na prompt fails cleanly
### INPUT ###
ok = confirm("Actually asked")
print(ok)
### ARGS ###
--reply-na
1
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDERR ###
error[RAD20047]: The confirm "Actually asked" was reached, but --reply-na said it wouldn't be.
  That's an assertion, not a fallback, so rad won't guess an answer
  --> <script>:1:6
  |
1 | ok = confirm("Actually asked")
  |      ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
2 | print(ok)
  |
  = info: rad docs RAD20047
### EXIT ###
1

### TITLE ###
A loop consumes one answer per pass and stops when they run out
### INPUT ###
for item in ["a", "b", "c"]:
    ok = confirm("Delete {item}?")
    print(item, ok)
### ARGS ###
--reply
2:y
--reply
2:n
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDOUT ###
a true
b false
### STDERR ###
error[RAD20047]: The confirm "Delete c?" ran out of answers - it executed more times than --reply
  accounted for. Repeat the flag once per pass; rad won't reuse the last answer
  --> <script>:2:10
  |
1 | for item in ["a", "b", "c"]:
2 |     ok = confirm("Delete {item}?")
  |          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
3 |     print(item, ok)
  |
  = info: rad docs RAD20047
### EXIT ###
1

### TITLE ###
Options computed at runtime are only checked when reached
### INPUT ###
fn servers():
    return ["alpha", "beta"]
choice = pick(servers())
print(choice)
### ARGS ###
--reply
3:gamma
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDERR ###
error[RAD20047]: "gamma" is not one of the options for the pick "Pick an option". Options: alpha,
  beta. Answers must match exactly - rad won't guess which one you meant
  --> <script>:3:10
  |
2 |     return ["alpha", "beta"]
3 | choice = pick(servers())
  |          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
4 | print(choice)
  |
  = info: rad docs RAD20047
### EXIT ###
1

### TITLE ###
A multipick answer is comma-separated with escapes
### INPUT ###
picks = multipick(["a,b", "c", "d"], min=2)
print(picks)
### ARGS ###
--reply
1:a\,b,c
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDOUT ###
[ "a,b", "c" ]

### TITLE ###
A multipick answer below min is rejected
### INPUT ###
picks = multipick(["a", "b", "c"], min=2)
print(picks)
### ARGS ###
--reply
1:a
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDERR ###
error[RAD20046]: the multipick at 1 needs at least 2 selections, but the answer gave 1
  = info: rad docs RAD20046
### EXIT ###
7

### TITLE ###
A secret input cannot be answered on the command line
### INPUT ###
pw = input("Password", secret=true)
print(pw)
### ARGS ###
--reply
1:hunter2
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDERR ###
error[RAD20046]: cannot answer the secret input "Password" at 1 from the command line: arguments are
  visible to other processes on this machine. Run it where there's a terminal, or use --reply-na 1
  if this run won't reach it
  = info: rad docs RAD20046
### EXIT ###
7

### TITLE ###
A local shadowing a builtin is not a prompt
### INPUT ###
fn pick(opts):
    return opts[0]
choice = pick(["a", "b"])
print(choice)
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDOUT ###
a

### TITLE ###
confirm-shell has no terminal to ask at
### INPUT ###
$`echo hi`
### ARGS ###
--confirm-shell
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDERR ###
error: --confirm-shell asks you to approve every shell command, but there's no terminal to ask at.

  Drop --confirm-shell, or run this where a terminal is available.
### EXIT ###
7

### TITLE ###
An author-marked confirm shell command is answerable
### INPUT ###
confirm $`echo ran-it`
print("after")
### ARGS ###
--reply
1:yes
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDOUT ###
after

### TITLE ###
Declining an author-marked confirm shell command skips it
### INPUT ###
confirm $`echo ran-it`
print("after")
### ARGS ###
--reply
1:no
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDERR ###
error[RAD20000]: Command exited with code 1
  --> <script>:1:1
  |
1 | confirm $`echo ran-it`
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
2 | print("after")
  |
  = info: rad docs RAD20000
### EXIT ###
1

### TITLE ###
An answer reaches its prompt through every call shape
### INPUT ###
fn ask_deep():
    return input("deep")
h = ask_deep
a = input("plain")
b = ["x", "y"].pick_kv(["vx", "vy"])
c = "interp {a}".input()
d = h()
print(a, b, c, d)
### ARGS ###
--reply
4:A
--reply
5:x
--reply
6:C
--reply
2:D
### STDOUT ###
A vx C D

### TITLE ###
A prompt in a loop is flagged and takes one answer per pass
### INPUT ###
for x in ["a", "b"]:
    ok = confirm("Delete {x}?")
    print(x, ok)
### ARGS ###
--reply
2:yes
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDOUT ###
a true
### STDERR ###
error[RAD20047]: The confirm "Delete b?" ran out of answers - it executed more times than --reply
  accounted for. Repeat the flag once per pass; rad won't reuse the last answer
  --> <script>:2:10
  |
1 | for x in ["a", "b"]:
2 |     ok = confirm("Delete {x}?")
  |          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
3 |     print(x, ok)
  |
  = info: rad docs RAD20047
### EXIT ###
1

### TITLE ###
A filtered pick that resolves itself still consumes its answer in order
### INPUT ###
opts = ["alpha", "beta"]
for f in ["alpha", "", ""]:
    x = pick(opts, f)
    print(f, x)
### ARGS ###
--reply
3:alpha
--reply
3:beta
--reply
3:alpha
### STDOUT ###
alpha alpha
 beta
 alpha

### TITLE ###
A secret input is refused even when secret is computed at runtime
### INPUT ###
is_secret = true
pw = input("Password", secret=is_secret)
print(pw)
### ARGS ###
--reply
2:hunter2
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDERR ###
error[RAD20047]: The secret input "Password" needs a terminal. It can't be answered with --reply,
  because command-line arguments are visible to other processes. Run this where a terminal is
  available, or use --reply-na if this run shouldn't reach it
  --> <script>:2:6
  |
1 | is_secret = true
2 | pw = input("Password", secret=is_secret)
  |      ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
3 | print(pw)
  |
  = info: rad docs RAD20047
### EXIT ###
1

### TITLE ###
A multipick answer may select nothing when min allows it
### INPUT ###
sel = multipick(["a", "b"], min=0)
print(sel)
### ARGS ###
--reply
1:
### STDOUT ###
[ ]

### TITLE ###
A multipick answer may not select the same option twice
### INPUT ###
sel = multipick(["a", "b", "c"], min=2, max=2)
print(sel)
### ARGS ###
--reply
1:a,a
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDERR ###
error[RAD20046]: "a" is selected twice for the multipick at 1; list each option at most once
  = info: rad docs RAD20046
### EXIT ###
7

### TITLE ###
A pick that resolves itself consumes its answer but does not fight it
### INPUT ###
opts = ["only"]
print("side effect")
x = pick(opts)
print(x)
### ARGS ###
--reply
3:something
### STDOUT ###
side effect
only

### TITLE ###
A filtered pick with a choice left gets a blank like any other
### INPUT ###
x = pick(["alpha", "beta"], "a")
print(x)
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDERR ###
error[RAD20046]: this script needs to ask you something, but there's no terminal

  TestCase prompts in 1 place. rad found no terminal - stdin isn't one and /dev/tty isn't available
  - so it can't ask. Supply the answers up front, keyed by line number.

  TestCase:1  pick options: alpha, beta  filtered - may not prompt, and then ignores its answer

    rad TestCase --color=never --reply '1:<option>'

  Every <...> is a blank - rad writes the shape of the command, never the answers. Fill each one in;
  rad takes the text literally and matches it exactly. For a prompt on a branch you know won't run,
  or a filtered pick you expect to settle itself, use --reply-na instead of choosing for it.
  = info: rad docs RAD20046
### EXIT ###
7

### TITLE ###
An interactive builtin used as a value cannot be answered at all
### INPUT ###
f = pick
print("side effect")
print(f(["alpha", "beta"]))
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDERR ###
error[RAD20046]: this script needs to ask you something, but there's no terminal

  TestCase prompts in 1 place. rad found no terminal - stdin isn't one and /dev/tty isn't available
  - so it can't ask. Supply the answers up front, keyed by line number.

  TestCase:1  pick used as a value - rad can't see where it runs; assert with --reply-na

    rad TestCase --color=never --reply-na 1

  = info: rad docs RAD20046
### EXIT ###
7

### TITLE ###
An empty input answer takes the declared default
### INPUT ###
env = input("Env", default="prod")
print(env)
### ARGS ###
--reply
1:
### STDOUT ###
prod

### TITLE ###
A builtin used as a value is listed and can be asserted away
### INPUT ###
handlers = {"interactive": pick, "auto": fn(o) o[0]}
h = handlers["auto"]
print("side effect")
print(h(["alpha", "beta"]))
### ARGS ###
--reply-na
1
### STDOUT ###
side effect
alpha

### TITLE ###
A builtin used as a value cannot take a reply
### INPUT ###
f = pick
print(f(["alpha", "beta"]))
### ARGS ###
--reply
1:alpha
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDERR ###
error[RAD20046]: cannot answer the pick used as a value at 1: the script passes it around as a value
  rather than calling it, so rad can't tell where it runs and has nothing to attach an answer to.
  Use --reply-na 1 if this run won't invoke it
  = info: rad docs RAD20046
### EXIT ###
7

### TITLE ###
A pick that can only land on one option is answered with reply-na
### INPUT ###
x = pick(["zulu", "beta", "gamma"], "be")
y = pick(["solo"])
print(x, y)
### NO_TERMINAL ###
### STDERR ###
error[RAD20046]: this script needs to ask you something, but there's no terminal

  TestCase prompts in 2 places. rad found no terminal - stdin isn't one and /dev/tty isn't available
  - so it can't ask. Supply the answers up front, keyed by line number.

  TestCase:1  pick options: zulu, beta, gamma  settles on "beta" - won't ask
  TestCase:2  pick options: solo  settles on "solo" - won't ask

    rad TestCase --color=never --reply-na 1 --reply-na 2

  = info: rad docs RAD20046
### EXIT ###
7

